title The fold that hid the link it broke, and the four guards that agreed to look away
prompt The baton -- open the lap, read the card whole, take the next agent-doable work reds-first.
rota lap 4053, row 3 -- Water, Venus, care and flow, and its sense is taste. Read the threshold and all three seats: the shape you grew and the shape you are, the happy zone and the thin edge, growing a language. The threshold's own discipline chose the lap's method: taste only works up close, so read the actual file and run the actual witness. It changed the answer four separate times.
think Opened by reading construction/ITINERARY.md whole. The GRASS block named %474 BOOKED -- the live front folds by hand with nothing re-anchoring its links, and the loom is the booked lap. Took it.
think Measured the class before building for it, rather than building to the sixteen instances %474 had named. The row's own repair was bounded to five shelves; the class across the whole room read 345 across 276 shelves, and reaches back to July, since both season ledgers are construction/TASKS.md and construction/ROADMAP.md folded down one directory in 20260724.
think Read up close rather than trusting the row's count of blind guards, and it changed. The row named three; there are four, and living_docs_lint is the one it missed -- which matters, because tracked_link explicitly hands the broken-link job to it. Reading the roster it actually uses showed zero construction/archive rows.
think Then the reason underneath them turned out to be one reason rather than four. tracked_link reads past any stamped basename; readme_reach calls a file living only when its basename carries no stamp, and merely reports the rest; both stop at the same property. A fold shelf's basename always carries a stamp, so a fold's own output wears the exact property that makes every link instrument let it go.
think Asked whether gating testimony contradicts a seated decision, since readme_reach declines to ratchet it on purpose. It does not, and the distinction is mechanical rather than a judgment call: a stale reference was correct when written and broke when its target moved, and it resolves under neither correction; a depth-lost link was never correct in the file it sits in, and resolves under exactly one. The gate's subject is defined by the property that separates it.
think Checked whether ambiguity was possible before writing a counter for it. Correction A wants a target beginning archive/, correction B one beginning ../ -- disjoint prefixes, so a target matches at most one. That is a stronger claim than the measurement's ambig=0, and it means the scan carries no ambiguity counter, since a counter that can never fire teaches a reader something false about the risk.
think My first crude extractor read 93 instances. Reading two of them up close showed they were a row QUOTING a broken link as its own subject, inside backticks -- prose about a link, which Markdown renders literally. Stripping code spans and fixing the target pattern moved the real count to 345.
think Wrote the repointer rather than a sed because a blanket rewrite cannot tell the classes apart: one shelf held 288 depth-lost links beside 401 stale ones, and a blanket ](../ -> ](../../ would have turned 401 honest breaks into 401 differently-wrong ones. That is guessing, at scale, in testimony.
think Verified the repair moved no prose rather than asserting it. With every ](target) masked to ](@), all four files hash identically before and after -- so only link targets changed, and not one word of testimony moved.
obs The fault fired a fourth time while I was reading about it. Three of the 345 sit on a shelf written at 09:23 this morning, after the %474 repair at 09:09 -- so the mechanism was written down, published, and then repeated within fifteen minutes by a hand that had just described it. That is the row's own claim arriving as evidence rather than as argument.
obs A red of my own, inside the pen, and it is the sharper half. My first control spelled an absence with the presence helper and printed no_shelves_never_ok=no -- an assertion whose NAME claims one thing while its VALUE means the opposite. A witness gates that as failure and a reader gates it as nothing at all. An absence has its own word now, and the same slip would have read green in a control nobody re-read.
obs And the same phase could not reach the state it meant to build: git rm of the last shelf takes the directory with it, so the scan answered no_room where the pen wanted no_shelves. Both are correct refusals at exit 2, which is exactly why it passed unnoticed at first -- a pen that cannot reach a state proves nothing about it, however green it reads.
obs The two instruments proved each other without being asked to. Folding %481 and %484 to a shelf ran reds_fold.sh, whose re-anchoring is this lap's own subject, and the new guard then read that fresh shelf clean from outside. The loom's claim and an independent reading agreed.
obs Booked %486 rather than %485: --next read 486 after a fetch, because a peer published 485 while this lap ran. The monotone guard reads one gap locally and closes it on the rebase, which is the derived-spine law working rather than a collision.
obs Named with a number rather than swept. The five rooms that fold two levels deep carry the same class wanting ../../../: sampled at most 400 files per room, active-designing 730, counsel 99, expanding-prompts 34, waymarks 27 -- at least 890 instances. That is its own campaign, and a figure is what makes it weighable rather than a shrug.
obs The guard found a red in ITSELF on its first real use, which is the best thing that happened this lap. Mid-rebase, git ls-files listed the shelf I had just renamed and the awk died with fatal: cannot open file -- so the reading that exists for a tree in motion could not survive one. Absent listed paths are skipped and counted now, and a pen proves it by deleting a committed shelf. A guard is not finished when it passes; it is finished when it has been run somewhere awkward.
obs The fourth duplication of the day, and mine this time. I folded %481 and %484 to one shelf; a peer folded %481 to its own shelf while this lap ran, so two shelves would have carried one row. Theirs was published and mine was not, so mine gave the row back and kept %484 alone -- the same rule the %484 lap applied to its own guard yesterday. Nothing in the tree publishes what a lap is holding, which is the card's open question asked a fourth time.
obs %440 fired on BOTH my rebases -- its tenth and eleventh -- and the peer commit directly beneath mine is literally titled re-sort the shelf the rebase reordered, so it fired on someone else in the same hour. It is a fleet-wide tax on every rebase now, not one seat's habit. Its tenth firing was exactly where the last lap said it would -- the shelf re-sorts after the final rebase, where the nib is. Upstream's 115056 row merged above my 122329 one and broke newest-first; one sort by stamp descending moved one line and index_row_bound read ok. The guard caught it in the hot pass rather than after the push, which is the difference between this firing and the ninth.
obs The one red in both hot passes is %483 and nothing else, and I proved it from the card rather than assuming it. Every row in construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri carries one identical stamp, so the card is written wholesale at a pass's close; standing_equipment therefore read index_row_bound red from the pass BEFORE the repair, while index_row_bound itself ran green at 2s in the same pass. The card now holds it green, so the lag is spent. The card is gitignored and ships nothing.
obs A slip of my own in the instrument, not the work. I piped the first hot pass through tail -30, which threw away the guard list and left me with a count of one red and no name for it -- I had to recover it from the run card. A pass whose output is truncated to its summary is a pass that cannot answer the one question its summary raises.
